WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.830 --> 00:00:06.470
When it comes to deploying ORv3 in the real world,

2
00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:10.770
two major players, Meta and Google, have each taken unique

3
00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:14.890
approaches, reflecting their scale, data center infrastructure, and

4
00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:19.110
evolving workload demands. These exercise different options in the

5
00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:22.830
ORv3 -based specification. Let's start with Meta.

6
00:00:23.530 --> 00:00:27.710
Meta's implementation of ORv3 builds on the standard specifications,

7
00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:31.590
option one with key adaptations for high performance computing.

8
00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:37.510
Their rack design emphasizes high availability and mechanical consistency

9
00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:41.750
using standardized 21 -inch IT gear form factors.

10
00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.350
It can support 44 OU total or 47 RU total

11
00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.070
units in terms of its height for payloads.

12
00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:53.730
It enables streamlined airflow and simplified servicing across global

13
00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:59.230
facilities. Power is delivered via 48V busbars, and the

14
00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:03.410
design accommodates high power workloads with modularity for future

15
00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:09.030
growth. Meta's ORv3 racks also include extended monitoring

16
00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:13.730
capabilities and predictable front -to -back airflow profiles, helping

17
00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:18.030
their infrastructure support rapid scalability while managing thermal

18
00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:24.330
loads efficiently. An update to Meta's frame specification with

19
00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:28.010
also planned and later came with high power rack versions

20
00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:31.410
and Meta's Catalina project and OCP contribution.

21
00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:37.290
On the other hand, Google's implementation offers depth options of

22
00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:39.770
both 32 and 48 inches.

23
00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:42.630
It has an OCP compliant feature set.

24
00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:48.050
It's capable of integrating industry standard equipment designed for

25
00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:51.730
EIA 19 -inch racks as well as custom payloads that

26
00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.910
are up to 24 inches wide and even greater than

27
00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:58.830
36 inches deep. Furthermore, it has a load rating of

28
00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:02.790
3 ,000 pounds which is on par with ORv3 specification

29
00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.570
of approximately 1 ,400 kilograms.

30
00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:11.670
The Google ORv3 implementation provides rear accessible flexible cable

31
00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:15.550
management as well as the ability to integrate both 48

32
00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:19.370
volt DC power distribution as well as AC PDUs.

33
00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:24.370
Finally, there are provisions for rear and front accessories as

34
00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:27.430
well as the option for rack level EMI containment.

35
00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:32.970
What unites both companies is their commitment to open standards

36
00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:37.970
and the scalability that ORv3 provides, whether the goal is

37
00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:41.330
performance, sustainability, or deployment speed.

38
00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:47.610
ORv3 allows organizations to standardize their mechanical and electrical

39
00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:52.430
infrastructure, adapt to liquid cooling, and support both AI and

40
00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:57.330
cloud native workloads. Their contributions are also reflected in

41
00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.610
the spec documents that help evolve the ORv3 standard.

42
00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:07.130
Meta, Google, and others continue to iterate on ORv3 through

43
00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:10.430
collaborative development and transparent publishing.

44
00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:16.490
From hyperscale data centers to AI ready deployments, ORv3 is

45
00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:19.570
